erdict": "supported", "evidence": ["investigate"], "finding": "The line had 2.5 h outside limits on 6 Jan versus 1.25 h on 5 Jan; demand was the main cause in all 3 episodes on each date. On 6 Jan, demand near the line contributed 59.5\u201384.5% and demand elsewhere 24.4\u201334.6% in the investigated episode changes; on 5 Jan, the corresponding local-demand shares were 43.7\u201376.5% and elsewhere 17.9\u201331.4%.", "factor": "Demand near Bus_0 -> Bus_1 [L0] (8 buses)"}, {"hypothesis": "Generator output was the main reason the line stayed overloaded longer on 6 January.", "verdict": "refuted", "evidence": ["investigate"], "finding": "Unit output was not the main cause on either date: it counteracted two 6 Jan episode changes (\u221219.1% and \u22120.5%) and contributed 16.1% in the longest episode; on 5 Jan it contributed in one episode (38.4%), while demand remained the main cause in all three.", "factor": "Units' output (5 units)"}], "counts": {"supported": 1, "refuted": 1, "untested": 0}, "tool_suggestions": [], "data_suggestions": [], "note": "In the answer: the supported hypotheses as findings, the refuted ones as ruled out, and the untested ones as not tested \u2014 with the tool that would be needed \u2014 never as findings."}}], "tool_error_count": 0, "model_calls": 7, "prompt_tokens": [28229, 28471, 28792, 30310, 31935, 33189, 34232], "output_tokens": [212, 26, 362, 469, 846, 588, 603], "cached_tokens": [28199, 28226, 28468, 28789, 30307, 31932, 33186], "assistant": "The line from the external grid, **Bus_0\u2013Bus_1 [L0]**, was overloaded for **2.5 hours on 6 January**, compared with **1.25 hours on 5 January**. The difference was mainly a demand-driven evening overload: on 6 January, the longest episode ran from **19:15 to 21:00** and peaked at **134.1%** loading. On 5 January, the overloads were shorter, with a peak of **107.6%**.\n\nDemand was identified as the main cause in all three episodes on both days. In the 6 January episodes, demand near the line contributed **59.5\u201384.5%** of the change into overload, with demand elsewhere contributing **24.4\u201334.6%**. The unit-output contribution varied; it was not the main explanation for the longer duration.\n\nThese percentages describe contributions to the change at each episode\u2019s onset, not a direct comparison of total demand between the two dates. Reactive demand was estimated using the default **0.95 power factor**.\n\n\ud83d\udccb Data source: measurements"}
